{"cases":[{"attack":true,"case":"The proposal's central safety claim is that committed roots stay valid 'provided the edge operator is root-preserving,' but this is offered as a conditional guard rather than a demonstrated invariant. The edge operator materializes attack-edges from warrant-carriage, and the closure rule iterates this to a least fixpoint. For root validity to be preserved, one must show that no sequence of closure-rule applications can ever introduce an attack-edge whose source is a committed root. The target never argues this; it merely stipulates it. A single closure step could compose a carried warrant from a non-root node into an attack on a committed root, and unless monotonicity is shown to be *source-restricting* (not merely order-preserving), the least fixpoint will deterministically include that edge, silently invalidating the root. Thus the proposal preserves determinism and polynomial cost but does not establish — and on its face may violate — the validity-of-every-committed-root guarantee it was required to preserve.","counterexample":[{"structure":"Bipartite warrant graph: nodes {R, A, B}; R is a committed root (self-warranted, no incoming attacks initially). A carries a warrant attacking B. B carries a warrant attacking R. The closure rule propagates carried warrants: since B's warrant attacks R, after one closure step an attack-edge B→R is materialized. The least fixpoint of the edge operator includes edge(B,R). R now has an incoming attack-edge from a suspended node, so R is no longer unattacked and its root validity is compromised — even though the operator is monotone and deterministic."}],"target":"c08594c10a414309047f9fa543ead03f028bb6260264dafc3e784e8567cc477d"}]}